<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lakshadweep</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laccadive Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lakshadweep is a group of coral islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, forming a union territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Formerly called the Laccadive Islands in English, most of them were long held by the Kolathiri rulers and then the Arakkal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a>, until the British took control in the 19th century. Most of its people are Muslims speaking Malayalam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakshadweep.jpg" alt="Lakshadweep" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manvendra Bhangui from Chennai, India · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_beach_side_resort_at_Kadmat_Island,_Lakshadweep.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lakshadweep is an archipelago of 36 islands and islets, with 12 atolls, three reefs and five submerged banks, lying 220 to 440 km off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>. Only ten are inhabited, and the total land area is about 32.62 square kilometres. There are three subgroups: the Amindivi Islands in the north, the Laccadive Islands, and the atoll of Minicoy south of the Nine Degree Channel. The name means one hundred thousand islands in Malayalam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and Laccadive appears to be its anglicised form. The islands lie at the northern end of the Chagos-Lakshadweep Ridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence from Kalpeni shows settlement from at least 1500 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> mentions the islands as a source of turtle shell, and the geographer Ptolemy names some of them, including Kavaratti, Agatti, Amini and Minicoy. Finds from Kadamath and Androth show trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from the 2nd century CE. Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam literature</a> says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> ruled the region. Local tradition credits Cheraman Perumal, the last Chera king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, with the first settlement, though no historical evidence supports it, and holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> came with Ubaidullah in 661 CE.</p><p>Later the islands passed to the Pallavas, the Kadambas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a> captured the region in 1018-19. In the late 15th century the Portuguese took control to obtain coir; the islanders revolted and expelled them in 1545. Most islands then came to the Arakkal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Kannur</a>, while the Amindivi Islands came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. The Arakkal family held the rest in return for an annual tribute to the British, who later took over the administration, citing unpaid taxes. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947 the islands became a union territory in 1956 and were renamed Lakshadweep on 1 November 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lakshadweep is administered as a single district, with Kavaratti as its capital since 1964. English is the official language. Jeseri, a dialect of Malayalam, is widely spoken, and Mahl, a dialect of Dhivehi, on Minicoy. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 64,473 people, about 96.6 per cent of them Muslim, and most are descended from migrants from the Malabar coast. The earliest settlers were probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, as the social stratification and matrilineal kinship suggest. Fishing, coconut growing and tourism are the main occupations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lakshadweep” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It covered the southernmost part of the Indian subcontinent: present-day Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Puducherry, <b>Lakshadweep</b> and southern parts of Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 16th century, as the Portuguese disrupted the coast, they became an autonomous power, holding the title Ali Raja, lord of the sea, and ruling the town and the <b>Lakshadweep</b> islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/arakkal-kingdom">Arakkal kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
